Colic
Paediatric & Neonatal
Inconsolable crying >3 hours/day, Evening symptom clustering, Normal growth/examination

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: PDN-012
Reason for Visit: Unrelenting, inconsolable crying in an infant for four hours causing severe caregiver distress and concern for acute physical injury or intussusception.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and parents
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage documented an infant with continuous, high-pitched, piercing crying and a tense abdomen. Immediate assessment was required to definitively exclude strangulated hernias, hair tourniquet syndrome, or acute abdominal pathology prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "He has been screaming non-stop since 4 PM, he is pulling his legs up, and absolutely nothing will calm him down."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with recurrent, prolonged, and unexplainable periods of severe crying. The caregivers describe a rule of threes pattern: crying for more than three hours today, occurring most days of the week, starting precisely when the infant was three weeks old. The episodes predictably cluster in the late afternoon and early evening hours. The crying is high-pitched, piercing, and inconsolable. The parents note physical signs of hypertonia strictly during the episode, including clenched fists and drawn-up legs, along with facial flushing. Feeding patterns and interval behaviors during the day have been normal.
Functional Status: Currently highly distressed, vigorously crying.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe caregiver exhaustion, frustration, and high risk of non-accidental trauma (Shaken Baby Syndrome) due to extreme psychological stress.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: Not measured due to distress | HR: 155 | Temp: 36.9C | RR: 45 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 4.5 Kg (50th percentile)
General: Vigorously crying infant, flushed face, consolable momentarily with feeding but quickly resumes crying.
Physical Exam: Comprehensive physical examination is completely normal. The abdomen is visually distended during crying but palpates soft between breaths with no masses, guarding, or organomegaly. No inguinal hernias identified. Complete inspection of all digits and genitalia reveals no hair tourniquets. Tympanic membranes are clear. Fontanelle is flat. Sclerae are white. The diagnostic tetrad of inconsolable crying >3 hours/day, evening symptom clustering, and a normal examination is confirmed.
Environmental Observations: Parents appear tearful, overwhelmed, and visibly shaking.
Medication Reconciliation: Simethicone drops trialed with zero efficacy.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Colic - Status: Active episode
Differentials:

Intussusception - Ruled out due to the daily chronicity of the evening crying, soft abdomen without a sausage-shaped mass, and absence of bloody stools.

Incarcerated Inguinal Hernia - Ruled out by direct visual and palpable examination of the groin revealing completely normal anatomy.

Hair Tourniquet Syndrome - Ruled out by complete undressing and meticulous inspection of all digits and penile appendages.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High (Risk of caregiver burnout/non-accidental injury)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The infant's acute presentation of unrelenting, high-pitched crying accompanied by physical posturing necessitated a rapid, after-hours clinical assessment. Telephone triage accurately identified the critical need to exclude occult physical pain or a surgical abdomen, which cannot be diagnosed over the phone. Objective clinical examination confirmed an absolutely normal physical state, definitively ruling out organic pathology and securing the diagnosis of infant colic. Delaying this assessment would have subjected the parents to unbearable psychological strain and dangerously increased the risk of non-accidental trauma due to frustration. Immediate physical evaluation provided absolute diagnostic clarity and allowed for critical parental reassurance and de-escalation.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Reassurance provided. Emphasized that the infant is completely healthy and experiencing no physical harm.
Positive Behavioral Change: When the crying escalates and frustration peaks, physically place the infant safely in their cot, close the door, and walk away to drink a glass of water for exactly 5 minutes. This programmed disengagement immediately breaks the cycle of parental panic and prevents the impulsive, dangerous reaction of shaking the infant, reinforcing the concept that a crying baby in a safe crib is not a medical emergency.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Advised utilizing community child health nurse support lines.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 1 week to monitor parental coping and infant growth.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the infant develops a fever (>38.0C), begins vomiting green fluid, passes blood in the stool, or becomes unusually floppy and difficult to wake.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Colic is a diagnosis of exclusion based on a normal exam; organic causes can develop independently at any time.
Clinical Course: The crying predictably peaks at 6-8 weeks of age and almost universally resolves spontaneously by 3 to 4 months of age. There are no long-term negative developmental effects.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency medical attention if the child feels unusually hot, has a fit, or if you feel you are losing control and might harm the baby.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Colic is a developmental stage of extreme fussiness, not a disease. The baby's nervous system is easily overwhelmed at the end of the day. The clenched fists and pulled-up legs are a reaction to the crying, not the cause of it.
Actionable Steps: Tag-team the crying hours with another adult if possible. Use white noise or gentle rocking, but accept that sometimes the baby will simply cry despite all interventions.

